from extras import ex

ex('safety',"Hazard symbols warn you about a danger, such as flammable or corrosive. A risk assessment lists the hazards, how likely harm is, and how to reduce it. Tie back long hair and stand up when heating.",
 [('Which symbol warns that a substance can burn skin?','Corrosive.','Flammable.','Harmful to the environment.','Corrosive substances can damage skin and eyes on contact.'),
  ('What should you do first if a chemical splashes in your eye?','Rinse it with lots of clean water and tell your teacher.','Rub your eye hard.','Wait until the lesson ends.','Rinsing quickly washes the chemical away before it does more damage.'),
  ('Why should you stand up during practical work with a Bunsen burner?','So you can move away quickly if something spills or catches fire.','So you can see the board better.','Because chairs are flammable.','Standing lets you step back from hot spills or flames.'),
  ('A risk assessment is written…','before you start the practical.','after the practical is finished.','only if something goes wrong.','You plan how to stay safe before you begin.')],
 [('What is a hazard?','Something that could cause harm.'),
  ('Give two safety rules for heating a liquid in a test tube.','Wear safety goggles.~Point the test tube away from people / heat gently / use a test-tube holder.'),
  ('Explain how a risk assessment helps keep a practical safe.','It identifies the hazards.~It judges how likely and how serious harm could be.~It sets out precautions to reduce the risk.')])

ex('variables',"A fair test changes only one variable at a time. If two things change at once, you cannot tell which one caused the result. A good question links the independent and dependent variables.",
 [('You test how the height of a ramp affects the speed of a car. What is the independent variable?','Height of the ramp.','Speed of the car.','Mass of the car.','You choose and change the ramp height.'),
  ('Which is a control variable in that test?','The same car is used each time.','The speed of the car.','The height of the ramp.','Keeping the car the same makes the test fair.'),
  ('Which prediction is testable?','The higher the ramp, the faster the car will go.','Cars are interesting.','The ramp is red.','A testable prediction says how one variable affects another.'),
  ('Why change only one variable at a time?','So you know what caused any change in results.','To make the test quicker.','So you need less equipment.','If two things change, you cannot tell which caused the effect.')],
 [('What is the dependent variable?','The variable you measure.'),
  ('Lia tests how temperature affects how fast sugar dissolves. Name the independent variable and one control variable.','Independent: temperature of the water.~Control: mass of sugar / volume of water / stirring.'),
  ('Explain why Lia must keep the volume of water the same.','Volume of water could also change the dissolving time.~If it changed, she could not tell whether temperature caused the effect.~Keeping it the same makes it a fair test.')])

ex('planning',"Choose a sensible range and interval, such as 20 °C to 60 °C in steps of 10 °C. Repeat readings and calculate a mean. Write a method in numbered steps.",
 [('Which instrument best measures 25 cm³ of water?','A measuring cylinder.','A beaker.','A thermometer.','A measuring cylinder has a scale for volume.'),
  ('How do you calculate a mean of three repeats?','Add the three values and divide by 3.','Choose the middle value only.','Subtract the smallest from the largest.','Mean = total ÷ number of values.'),
  ('A ruler marked in mm has a resolution of…','1 mm.','1 cm.','1 m.','It can show changes as small as 1 mm.'),
  ('Why should you repeat measurements?','To spot anomalies and get a more reliable mean.','To make the test unfair.','Because the first result is always wrong.','Repeats show whether results agree.')],
 [('What is meant by the resolution of an instrument?','The smallest change it can show.'),
  ('Times measured: 12.1 s, 12.5 s, 12.3 s. Calculate the mean time.','12.1 + 12.5 + 12.3 = 36.9 s.~36.9 ÷ 3 = 12.3 s.'),
  ('Explain why a stopwatch is better than counting seconds aloud.','A stopwatch has a better resolution / reads to 0.01 s.~Counting aloud is less precise and varies between people.~So the times are more accurate and repeatable.')])

ex('burner',"Light the burner with the air hole closed so you get the yellow safety flame. Open the air hole to get a blue roaring flame for heating. Never leave a lit burner unattended.",
 [('Which flame should be used when the burner is lit but not heating anything?','The yellow safety flame.','The blue roaring flame.','No flame.','The yellow flame is easy to see, so it is safer.'),
  ('Why is the blue flame hotter?','More oxygen mixes with the gas, so it burns more completely.','It uses a different gas.','Blue things are always hot.','Complete combustion transfers more energy.'),
  ('What should you place under the Bunsen burner?','A heatproof mat.','A sheet of paper.','A plastic tray.','A heatproof mat protects the bench.'),
  ('Which leaves soot on glassware?','The yellow flame.','The blue flame.','An electric heater.','Incomplete combustion in the yellow flame makes soot.')],
 [('What colour is the safety flame?','Yellow.'),
  ('Give two steps to light a Bunsen burner safely.','Close the air hole and put on goggles.~Light the splint/lighter first, then turn on the gas.'),
  ('Explain why the blue flame is better for heating than the yellow flame.','The air hole is open, so more oxygen is mixed in.~The gas burns more completely.~It is hotter and does not leave soot.')])

ex('data',"Plot the independent variable on the x-axis and the dependent variable on the y-axis. Draw a line or curve of best fit through the points. Do not join the dots with a ruler.",
 [('Which axis should show the independent variable?','The x-axis (horizontal).','The y-axis (vertical).','Either axis, it does not matter.','By convention, the variable you change goes along the bottom.'),
  ('What is a line of best fit?','A smooth line or curve that passes close to most points.','A line joining every point dot-to-dot.','A line through the first and last points only.','It shows the overall pattern.'),
  ('Which graph type suits "number of students with each eye colour"?','Bar chart.','Line graph.','Scatter graph with a curve.','Eye colour is a category, so use a bar chart.'),
  ('A table heading should include…','the quantity and its unit, e.g. Time (s).','only the unit.','nothing; numbers are enough.','Units belong in the heading, not after each number.')],
 [('What does a line of best fit show?','The overall pattern/trend in the results.'),
  ('A table has the heading "Temperature" and values 20, 40, 60. Give two ways to improve it.','Add the unit to the heading, e.g. Temperature (°C).~Add a column for the measured (dependent) variable / repeats and a mean.'),
  ('Explain how to plot a line graph of time against temperature.','Put temperature (independent) on the x-axis and time on the y-axis.~Choose even scales that use most of the grid and label axes with units.~Plot points accurately and draw a smooth line of best fit.')])

ex('evaluate',"A result is repeatable if you get similar results when you repeat it. It is reproducible if someone else gets similar results with different equipment. Improve a test by reducing errors, for example by using a data logger.",
 [('A thermometer reads 1 °C too high every time. This is…','a systematic error.','a random error.','an anomaly.','The error is the same each time, so it is systematic.'),
  ('What should you do with an anomalous result?','Repeat it and leave it out of the mean if it is clearly wrong.','Always include it in the mean.','Change the other results to match.','Anomalies can distort the mean.'),
  ('Which makes results more precise?','Using an instrument with better resolution.','Using a bigger beaker.','Writing faster.','Better resolution shows smaller differences.'),
  ('Results are reproducible when…','another person gets similar results.','you get one result only.','the results are all different.','Reproducible means others can repeat it and agree.')],
 [('What is an anomaly?','A result that does not fit the pattern.'),
  ('Repeat times were 5.2 s, 5.4 s and 8.9 s. Identify the anomaly and calculate the mean without it.','8.9 s is the anomaly.~(5.2 + 5.4) ÷ 2 = 5.3 s.'),
  ('Suggest and explain one improvement to an experiment timed with a hand-held stopwatch.','Use light gates / a data logger / film it.~This removes human reaction time.~So the timing is more accurate and repeatable.')])

ex('microscope',"Put the slide on the stage and use the coarse focus with the lowest-power lens first. Then use the fine focus for a sharp image. Stains, such as iodine, make cell parts easier to see.",
 [('Which lens should you use first?','The lowest-power objective lens.','The highest-power objective lens.','Any lens.','Low power makes it easier to find the specimen.'),
  ('Eyepiece ×10 and objective ×10. What is the total magnification?','×100.','×20.','×1.','10 × 10 = 100.'),
  ('Why is a stain added to cells?','To make cell parts easier to see.','To kill the cells.','To make the slide stick.','Stains add colour to parts such as the nucleus.'),
  ('Which part do you turn to make the image sharp at high power?','The fine focus.','The coarse focus.','The mirror.','The fine focus makes small adjustments.')],
 [('What does a microscope do?','Makes small objects look bigger (magnifies them).'),
  ('An eyepiece is ×5 and the objective is ×40. Calculate the total magnification.','Total magnification = 5 × 40.~= ×200.'),
  ('Describe how to prepare and view onion cells.','Peel a thin layer of onion skin and lay it flat on a slide.~Add a drop of iodine and lower a cover slip without trapping bubbles.~View on low power first, focus, then switch to higher power and use fine focus.')])

ex('cell',"Mitochondria are where most respiration happens, releasing energy. Ribosomes make proteins. The cell wall is made of cellulose and gives plant cells a fixed shape.",
 [('Where does most respiration happen in a cell?','Mitochondria.','Nucleus.','Cell wall.','Mitochondria release energy from glucose.'),
  ('Which part is found in plant cells but not animal cells?','Cell wall.','Cell membrane.','Cytoplasm.','Only plant cells have a cellulose cell wall.'),
  ('What does the cell membrane do?','Controls what enters and leaves the cell.','Makes food by photosynthesis.','Gives the cell a fixed shape.','The membrane is a barrier with control.'),
  ('Where do most chemical reactions take place in a cell?','Cytoplasm.','Vacuole.','Cell wall.','The cytoplasm is a jelly where reactions happen.')],
 [('Name the part of the cell that contains genetic information.','Nucleus.'),
  ('Name two parts found in plant cells but not animal cells.','Cell wall / chloroplasts / permanent vacuole.~Any second correct part.'),
  ('A cell has a nucleus, a cell wall and chloroplasts. Explain whether it is a plant or animal cell.','It is a plant cell.~Animal cells do not have a cell wall.~Chloroplasts are only found in plant (and algae) cells.')])

ex('specialised',"A sperm cell has a tail to swim and lots of mitochondria for energy. A nerve cell is long to carry signals around the body. Many leaf cells are packed with chloroplasts for photosynthesis.",
 [('Why does a red blood cell have no nucleus?','There is more space to carry oxygen.','So it can swim.','So it can photosynthesise.','No nucleus means more room for haemoglobin.'),
  ('Which cell has a tail?','Sperm cell.','Red blood cell.','Root hair cell.','The tail helps the sperm swim to the egg.'),
  ('Why is a nerve cell long?','To carry signals over long distances.','To store water.','To absorb light.','Long fibres link distant parts of the body.'),
  ('Why do root hair cells have no chloroplasts?','They are underground, with no light.','They are too small.','They are dead.','Without light, chloroplasts would be useless.')],
 [('What is a specialised cell?','A cell with features that help it do a particular job.'),
  ('Describe two adaptations of a sperm cell.','A tail for swimming.~Many mitochondria to release energy / a head with enzymes.'),
  ('Explain how the shape of a root hair cell helps the plant.','It has a long, thin extension.~This gives a large surface area.~So it can absorb more water and minerals from the soil.')])

ex('diffusion',"Diffusion is faster when it is warmer, because particles move faster. It is also faster when the concentration difference is bigger. Oxygen diffuses into cells and carbon dioxide diffuses out.",
 [('Which makes diffusion faster?','A higher temperature.','A lower temperature.','A smaller concentration difference.','Warmer particles move faster.'),
  ('Perfume spreads across a room by…','diffusion.','filtration.','condensation.','Perfume particles spread from high to low concentration.'),
  ('In which state is diffusion slowest?','Solid.','Gas.','Liquid.','Particles in solids cannot move from place to place.'),
  ('Oxygen moves into a cell because…','there is more oxygen outside than inside.','cells push it in.','oxygen is heavier.','Particles diffuse from high to low concentration.')],
 [('What is diffusion?','The spreading of particles from a high concentration to a low concentration.'),
  ('A tea bag is put in hot water and another in cold water. Predict which colours the water faster and why.','Hot water colours faster.~Particles move faster at a higher temperature.'),
  ('Explain how oxygen gets from the air in your lungs into your blood.','There is a higher concentration of oxygen in the air sacs than in the blood.~Oxygen particles diffuse from high to low concentration.~They pass through the thin walls into the blood.')])

ex('organisation',"The order of organisation is cells → tissues → organs → organ systems → organism. The heart is an organ made of muscle tissue and other tissues. The skeleton also makes blood cells in bone marrow.",
 [('Which is the correct order?','Cell, tissue, organ, organ system.','Organ, cell, tissue, system.','Tissue, cell, organ, system.','Each level is made from the one before.'),
  ('The stomach is an example of…','an organ.','a cell.','a tissue.','It is made of several tissues working together.'),
  ('Which is NOT a job of the skeleton?','Digesting food.','Support.','Protecting organs.','Digestion is done by the digestive system.'),
  ('Which bone protects the brain?','Skull.','Ribs.','Femur.','The skull surrounds the brain.')],
 [('What is a tissue?','A group of similar cells working together to do a job.'),
  ('Give two jobs of the skeleton.','Support / shape.~Protection / movement / making blood cells.'),
  ('Explain the difference between a tissue, an organ and an organ system.','A tissue is a group of similar cells.~An organ is made of different tissues working together.~An organ system is a group of organs working together, e.g. the digestive system.')])

ex('muscles',"The biceps bends the arm at the elbow and the triceps straightens it. Tendons join muscles to bones. Ligaments join bones to other bones at a joint.",
 [('Which muscle contracts to bend your arm?','Biceps.','Triceps.','Diaphragm.','The biceps pulls the forearm up.'),
  ('What joins a muscle to a bone?','Tendon.','Ligament.','Cartilage.','Tendons are tough cords linking muscle to bone.'),
  ('Why do muscles work in pairs?','Muscles can only pull, not push.','So one can rest.','Because bones push muscles.','One pulls each way.'),
  ('When the triceps contracts, the biceps…','relaxes.','also contracts.','pushes the arm.','Antagonistic pairs work opposite each other.')],
 [('What is an antagonistic pair?','Two muscles that work in opposite directions at a joint.'),
  ('Describe what happens to the biceps and triceps when you straighten your arm.','The triceps contracts.~The biceps relaxes.'),
  ('Explain why the elbow needs two muscles to move both ways.','Muscles can only pull when they contract.~The biceps pulls the forearm up to bend the arm.~The triceps is needed to pull it back down / straighten it.')])
